### === End-to-end gateway demo: per-IP rate limiting, simulated call from 2 separate machines using X-Forwarded-For header ===
### Run `docker compose up --build` on the host machine, then set @host to its LAN/reachable IP (not "localhost", unless testing from the host itself).
@host = 192.168.70.162

### Gateway builds keys as route:<path> (e.g. /login -> route:login) and sends key+caller IP separately to /check; rules are per-route only, no IP in keyPrefix.

### Step 1: seed a rule limiting /login to 3 req/60s per IP — keyPrefix must exactly equal the route key ("route:login", no trailing colon) or it will never match (always 429).
POST http://{{host}}:8080/api/v1/rules
X-Admin-Token: changeme
Content-Type: application/json

{"keyPrefix": "route:login", "limit": 3, "windowSeconds": 60}

### Step 2 (Machine A): run 3x for 200s counting down Remaining 2,1,0, then a 4th returns 429.
GET http://{{host}}:8082/login
X-Forwarded-For: 1.1.1.1

### Step 3 (Machine B): run the same request — it gets its own fresh quota (200, Remaining: 2), proving the two IPs track independently under one shared rule.
GET http://{{host}}:8082/login
X-Forwarded-For: 2.2.2.2

### Step 4 (optional): a route with no seeded rule is always denied (fail-closed), regardless of caller IP.
GET http://{{host}}:8082/does-not-exist

### Step 5 (optional, single machine): bypass the gateway and call /check directly with explicit ip= values to simulate two callers without a second machine.
GET http://{{host}}:8080/api/v1/rate-limit/check?key=route:login&ip=1.1.1.1

###
GET http://{{host}}:8080/api/v1/rate-limit/check?key=route:login&ip=2.2.2.2

### Cleanup: remove the seeded rule.
DELETE http://{{host}}:8080/api/v1/rules/route:login
X-Admin-Token: changeme
